 "not_evidence_of": "SYNTHETIC: in-memory FakeVenue accounts, synthetic data and worker threads in one process on one Windows workstation. Not venue evidence, not exchange-demo throughput, not a Linux certification, not a hosted-staging run.",
 "notes": [
  "Worker processes are threads of ONE process (4 trading workers x 4 account threads); separate-process takeover is measured by scripts/worker_kill_drill.py, not here.",
  "Lease TTL is a drill setting (5 s; production 60 s): takeover times scale with the TTL, not with N. A first run with a 1.5 s TTL showed why it must exceed the round: at 1,000 accounts a round took ~2 s on this loaded workstation, leases lapsed mid-round, the fence refused those steps and accounts moved between workers (fail-safe, but churn). fence_refused_lease_lapsed and accounts_moved_and_recovered_in_round record it.",
  "The request budget and the REST reconciliation / user-stream ledgers live in a throwaway legacy SQLite file on every dialect; the platform database is the dialect named in the row.",
  "tracemalloc peaks come from a separate pass (SQLite only) so that tracing does not inflate the timed stages.",
  "Queue orders are resting limit orders that never fill, so every position keeps exactly the protection it was given; the order-identity and lost-acknowledgement properties are the same as for a market order.",
  "Request budget: one egress address per venue and the repository's default limits and method weights (Binance VERIFIED_BY_DOC; Bybit and BingX UNVERIFIED configured envelopes). 'derived_*' fields are arithmetic on the measured window, not measurements.",
  "FakeVenue's own user-stream spec is an auth frame for every profile, which the Binance and BingX stream profiles cannot authenticate; StreamVenue supplies each venue's real spec shape. A test-double limitation.",
  "Other engineers' processes were running on the same workstation during the measurement: wall-clock figures include that noise."
 ],
